# Assignment 3 - Build Your Own Linux Shell Environment

## Scenario
The company wants every developer to have the same productive terminal environment, starting with a welcome screen that appears automatically every time a new terminal opens.

## Files
- welcome_screen_snippet.sh - a copy of the welcome screen code for documentation (the actual active code lives inside ~/.bashrc)

## How It Works
The welcome screen code was added to the end of ~/.bashrc. Since .bashrc runs automatically every time a new interactive terminal session starts, the welcome message is displayed without any manual action from the user.

## Welcome Screen Output
==========================================
      Welcome to ITI Linux Environment
==========================================

User        : <current username>
Hostname    : <machine hostname>
Date        : <current date and time>
Shell       : <path to login shell>
Current Dir : <current working directory>

Have a productive day!
==========================================

## Concepts Used
- Shell startup files (~/.bashrc)
- Command substitution: $(whoami), $(hostname), $(date), $(pwd)
- Environment variable: $SHELL
- echo for formatted terminal output

## Note
All displayed values (user, hostname, date, shell, directory) update automatically each time a new terminal is opened, since they are generated live using command substitution rather than hardcoded text.
